    {
      "id": "economy",
      "prose_links": [
        {
          "href": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris#Economy",
          "lang": "en",
          "text": "Economy"
        }
      ],
      "prose_text": {
        "href": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris#Economy",
        "html": "The economy of the City of Paris is based largely on services and commerce. Of the 390,480 enterprises in Paris, 80.6 percent are engaged in commerce, transportation, and diverse services, 6.5 percent in construction, and 3.8 percent in industry. The story is similar in the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris_Region\">Paris Region</a> (Île-de-France): 76.7 percent of enterprises are engaged in commerce and services, and 3.4 percent in industry.",
        "lang": "en",
        "local": true,
        "title": "Economy"
      },
      "title": {
        "href": "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q159810",
        "lang": "en",
        "text": "economy"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "connections",
      "prose_links": [],
      "prose_text": null,
      "title": {
        "href": "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q930933",
        "lang": "en",
        "text": "relation"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "history",
      "prose_links": [
        {
          "href": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris#History",
          "lang": "en",
          "text": "History"
        }
      ],
      "prose_text": {
        "href": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris#History",
        "html": "The <i><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parisii_(Gaul)\">Parisii</a></i> people inhabited the Paris area from around the middle of the 3rd century BC. One of the area's major north–south trade routes crossed the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seine\">Seine</a> on the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%8Ele_de_la_Cit%C3%A9\">Île de la Cité</a>, which gradually became an important trading centre. The Parisii traded with many river towns, some as far away as the Iberian Peninsula, and minted their own coins.",
        "lang": "en",
        "local": true,
        "title": "History"
      },
      "title": {
        "href": "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q309",
        "lang": "en",
        "text": "history"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "media",
      "prose_links": [
        {
          "href": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris#Culture",
          "lang": "en",
          "text": "Culture"
        }
      ],
      "prose_text": {
        "href": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris#Culture",
        "html": "For centuries, Paris has attracted artists from around the world. As a result, Paris has acquired a reputation as the \"City of Art\". Italian artists were a profound influence on the development of art in Paris in the 16th and 17th centuries, particularly in sculpture and reliefs. Painting and sculpture became the pride of the French monarchy, and the French royal family commissioned many Parisian artists to adorn their palaces during the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Baroque_and_Classicism\">French Baroque and Classicism</a> era. Sculptors such as <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fran%C3%A7ois_Girardon\">Girardon</a>, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antoine_Coysevox\">Coysevox</a>, and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicolas_Coustou\">Coustou</a> acquired reputations as the finest artists in the royal court in 17th-century France. <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pierre_Mignard\">Pierre Mignard</a> became the first painter to King <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Louis_XIV\">Louis XIV</a> during this period. In 1648, the <i><a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Acad%C3%A9mie_royale_de_peinture_et_de_sculpture\">Académie royale de peinture et de sculpture</a></i> (Royal Academy of Painting and Sculpture) was established to accommodate the dramatic interest in art in the capital.",
        "lang": "en",
        "local": true,
        "title": "Culture"
      },
